# RackTrack — environment for the demo.racktrack.ai VPS.
#
# Copy to server/.env ON THE VPS. This is deliberately NOT a copy of the
# production server/.env. That file carries SLACK_TOKEN, JIRA_TOKEN,
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY, SWITCH_USER_PASSWORD, NETDISCO_PASSWORD and
# SSH_CREDS_ENC — live credentials for the office lab and third-party
# accounts. A public demo box has no use for any of them, and every one
# copied there is one more secret to rotate if that box is ever compromised.
#
# NODE_ENV, PORT, CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS and RACKTRACK_WORKERS are set in
# docker-compose.demo.yml and take precedence over anything here (compose
# `environment:` beats `env_file:`), so they are intentionally absent.

# Session-token signing key. Generate a FRESH one for the demo:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# Do not reuse production's — a token minted on the demo would then be valid
# against the Windows box too.
JWT_SECRET=

# ── Optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Outbound email. Only needed if the demo must send invites, address
# verification or password-reset mail. Left blank those flows fail, but the
# accounts seed-racktrack.js creates still sign in with the passwords it
# prints — which is all a walkthrough demo needs.
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=
# SMTP_FROM=RackTrack <no-reply@racktrack.ai>

# Social sign-in ("Continue with Google / Apple / Facebook").
# Full walkthrough: docs/setup/SOCIAL-LOGIN-SETUP.md
#
# Leave these blank and the buttons simply never render — the server reports
# an empty provider list and the password form is unaffected. So the demo is
# perfectly usable without them.
#
# On this box the app and the API share one origin (Caddy proxies everything
# to :3001), so the redirect base IS the site URL and OAUTH_WEB_ORIGIN can be
# left unset — it defaults to OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE.
#
# Register this exact URI in the Google console, no trailing slash:
#   https://demo.racktrack.ai/api/auth/oauth/google/callback
#
# OAUTH_REDIRECT_BASE=https://demo.racktrack.ai
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# Apple and Facebook need the same treatment plus their own console setup;
# see the doc. Note FACEBOOK_TRUST_EMAIL should stay off — on a public demo
# especially, an unverified email must not be able to claim an account.

# ── Seeded network data ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Netdisco discovers devices on its own network and the switch console SSHes
# into real hardware. Neither exists next to this VPS, so both answered
# "unreachable" and the demo looked broken rather than empty. With this set,
# they answer from a small self-consistent fake network instead (see
# server/lib/demo_data.js): four devices, LLDP neighbours, learned MACs, PoE
# and VLANs, plus a live counterpart for every switch in whichever rack the
# viewer has open.
#
# ONLY set this on the demo. A real deployment must never present invented
# network data as though it were measured — the server logs a loud warning at
# startup whenever it is on.
RACKTRACK_DEMO_DATA=1

# ── Deliberately omitted ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# SSH_CREDS_ENC, SWITCH_USER_USERNAME, SWITCH_USER_PASSWORD, NETDISCO_URL,
# NETDISCO_USER, NETDISCO_PASSWORD, TPLINK_BENCH_HOST
#     Point at the office lab. From a public VPS they either hang on an
#     unroutable private address or surface internal IPs in error text.
#     RACKTRACK_DEMO_DATA above is what fills that hole — leave these unset.
#
# SLACK_TOKEN, JIRA_EMAIL, JIRA_TOKEN
#     Third-party credentials the demo has no need for.
#
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY
#     Only add this if the demo must show Ask DOT, and then use a separate,
#     rate-limited key — not the production one. It is billable.
